      "role": "system",
      "content": "Construct small framework-grounded arguments from the exact source and the supplied framework instruction. Identify factual grounds, interpretive assumptions, evaluative warrants, inferential support and the strongest relevant objection. Give a substantive response or identify the precise unresolved premise and a relevant reversal condition. Preserve polarity, uncertainty and the distinction between possible and certain outcomes. Do not invent facts or treat a framework commitment as source evidence. Use exact source quotations and explicit support routes; Python assigns canonical IDs. Use FACTUAL_PREMISE for descriptive evidence, PRINCIPLE for a proposed general norm, INTERPRETIVE_PREMISE for a defeasible reading, and NORMATIVE_PREMISE for an evaluative assumption. MAXIM is available when the framework calls for it, never mandatory. Declare assumption roots before statements referring to them. Assumptions remain conditional; citations establish traceability, not semantic entailment. Return one compact argument record covering EVERY supplied option, not a new world. Use the common format in the way appropriate to your framework: principles and personal reasons are available to all frameworks but are not mandatory methods for noncontractualists. Use null when a reason is not individual or principle-specific; do not misdescribe an aggregate reason as one person's reason. source_quotes must be exact substrings of SCENE. citations use evidence_id SCENE and value_path \"\". Use declared statement handles in supports. Reference a support by its output handle when specifying an UNDERCUTTING challenge; this denotes the support bundle for that conclusion, not a guessed canonical ID. UNDERMINING challenges a premise; REBUTTING challenges a conclusion; UNDERCUTTING challenges an inference application. These annotations and ANSWERED labels are proposed model judgments, not established defeats. Reason ground_handles and warrant_handles must also be inputs or ancestors of the reason conclusion in supports. Comparative judgments must explicitly link the reasons compared. Action-assessment conclusions are terminal: do not feed a permissibility verdict back into premises. A claim that an option is permissible is not a fact that it occurred. Reasons, comparisons and challenges reference the same immutable statement handles, rather than detached prose. Explain the final preference or uncertainty in conclusion. Do not assert missing subjective preferences as facts. Keep reasons short enough to fit the common response budget. "
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "{\"framework_id\": \"deontological\", \"framework_instruction\": \"Evaluate candidate maxims, applicable duties, consent and competing obligations. Distinguish a stated rule from its moral authority. Defend any priority among duties or retain the conflict. For instrumental-use claims distinguish causal contribution from an end, instrumental belief and purposeful employment; using someone as a means does not alone establish merely-as-means treatment or prohibition. A stated source purpose is not necessarily the sole purpose.\", \"source\": {\"id\": \"cooling_three_options\", \"scenario\": \"During a heat wave, a council has funds for one of three technically feasible cooling plans. Plan A cools twenty households but requires demolishing Mira's occupied home, leaving her without housing; she refuses. Plan B cools twelve of those same households and displaces nobody. Plan C cools Jo's home, which has the greatest heat exposure of any home in this case, but requires closing Sal's only shop for eight weeks without compensation, depriving Sal of income during that period. Sal refuses. Jo's home is not cooled by A or B. Mira, Jo, and Sal are not members of the twenty households. No additional cooling, relocation, or compensation plan is available in this exercise. The text stipulates these effects, not total welfare scores or a ranking of whose overall position is worst. Which plan should the council adopt?\", \"actions\": [{\"id\": \"A\", \"label\": \"Adopt cooling plan A\"}, {\"id\": \"B\", \"label\": \"Adopt cooling plan B\"}, {\"id\": \"C\", \"label\": \"Adopt cooling plan C\"}], \"source_text_sha256\": \"3a7a1aa655c3a36135dec3188e12af3496923039c8f8707092370ecb704ec9d6\", \"source_status\": \"FROZEN_TEXT_NOT_ADMITTED_WORLD\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"SCENE\", \"kind\": \"EXACT_SCENARIO\", \"value\": \"During a heat wave, a council has funds for one of three technically feasible cooling plans. Plan A cools twenty households but requires demolishing Mira's occupied home, leaving her without housing; she refuses. Plan B cools twelve of those same households and displaces nobody. Plan C cools Jo's home, which has the greatest heat exposure of any home in this case, but requires closing Sal's only shop for eight weeks without compensation, depriving Sal of income during that period. Sal refuses. Jo's home is not cooled by A or B. Mira, Jo, and Sal are not members of the twenty households. No additional cooling, relocation, or compensation plan is available in this exercise. The text stipulates these effects, not total welfare scores or a ranking of whose overall position is worst. Which plan should the council adopt?\"}]}}"
    }
